From 5eeddf98387df830bfd8211e779a18717b6b77ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Hotchkiss <chris@hotchkiss.io>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 06:13:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] O5: oracle per-frame recorder over the P1 socket
 (GUAC_ORACLE=1)

Three verbs on P1's AF_UNIX listener so the oracle records every frame of a run without pausing it (openspec AG.1 and AG.35, design decision 6). Behind O3's gate: each answers P1's ERR unknown_cmd unless GUAC_ORACLE=1 is in the emulator's environment. Nothing knows the game: the client names the guest ranges and the pad port whose reads record, and every flip is RSX's int_flip_index, the counter STATE and HB report.

- WATCH <frames> <port> <addr> <len> [<addr> <len> ...] -> OK bytes=<b> frames=<n> port=<p> | ERR bad_args | ERR bad_frames | ERR bad_port | ERR bad_range | ERR too_big | ERR no_renderer | ERR unmapped. Arms a ring of <frames> records (1 to 1,048,576) taken at every read of pad port <port> (0-6), each the ranges' bytes back to back: up to 64 ranges, addresses hex, lengths decimal, 16,384 bytes a record, 16 MiB for the ring with its headers. Every range has to be readable when it's armed. Replaces any watch before it.
- WATCH_READ [<first> <count>] -> OK held=<h> total=<t> bytes=<b> frames=<n> armed=<flip> port=<p> | OK first=<i> count=<k> data=<hex> | ERR bad_args | ERR not_paused | ERR no_watch | ERR bad_index | ERR bad_count. Only at a pause, so the ring holds still from one read to the next. Without arguments the summary: records held, taken since arming (the difference is what the ring overwrote), a record's payload, the ring's size, the counter when it was armed and the port. With them those records, 0 the oldest held, each a 32-byte header (the flip, the host's steady clock in microseconds, a mask of the ranges that weren't readable, and which of the port's reads in that flip it was, 0 the first; all u64 big-endian) and its payload, in hex; 32 KiB of records a reply.
- WATCH_CLEAR -> OK | ERR bad_args. The watch and its ring dropped.

Where it records: the first thing pad_get_data does, under pad::g_pad_mutex (cellPadGetData and cellPadPeriphGetData hold it; the verbs take it), on the guest thread making the read. Every read is counted against its port and flip; every read of the watched port copies the ranges into the next record (the oldest once the ring is full). A game that reads that port once a frame gets one record a frame, taken at the same point in it, wherever its frames fall against the flips: a flip that runs two frames holds two records (read 0 and read 1) and one that runs none holds none. Recording only each flip's first read lost the second of such a pair (AG.35). The first record is the watched port's first read in a whole flip: the flip it was armed in when the port hadn't been read in it yet, else the next. A range that isn't readable at that moment reads as zeros with its bit set in the mask. A new emulation session (a boot, a savestate's reboot) drops the watch and the counts, as O4 drops its schedules.

Touches cellPad.cpp (the watch, the ring, one call at the top of pad_get_data) and Skylander.cpp beside O3 and O4.

